Here is a complete JavaScript proposal with a mistake:
```javascript
function ticketPrice(price, isMember) {
  return price * 0.8;
}
console.log(ticketPrice(50, true));
console.log(ticketPrice(50, false));
console.log(ticketPrice(-5, true));
```
It prints 40, 40, and -4. It gives everyone a twenty-percent discount and accepts a negative price. The function never reads isMember. Passing one member example would miss those failures.

This separate complete example implements the stated twenty-percent member discount:
```javascript
function ticketPrice(price, isMember) {
  if (!Number.isFinite(price) || price < 0) {
    return null;
  }
  return isMember === true ? price * 0.8 : price;
}
console.log(ticketPrice(50, true));
console.log(ticketPrice(50, false));
console.log(ticketPrice(0, true));
console.log(ticketPrice(-5, true));
console.log(ticketPrice(50, 'false'));
```
The outputs are 40, 50, 0, null, and 50. null deliberately means invalid input. The ternary condition ? first : second chooses the discounted price only for exact true.

after:
This example applies the stated rule.

```javascript
function ticketPrice(price, isMember) {
  if (!Number.isFinite(price) || price < 0) return null;
  return isMember === true ? price * 0.9 : price;
}
console.log(ticketPrice(80, true));
console.log(ticketPrice(80, false));
console.log(ticketPrice(-5, true));
```

The outputs are 72, 80, and null.
